            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Hi!\r\n\r\nThe `config.autoread = true` means that the *latest* session will be loaded. As 'mini.sessions' provides functionality to save multiple sessions (with [`MiniSessions.write()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/6fffe2d106024c3c44cb0c686d168d3c7c0d5d80/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L144)), user might want to load some different (not latest) session. This is my personal workflow and the way it works together with default items of 'mini.starter'.\r\n\r\nSo the overall workflow of using multiple session usually goes like this:\r\n- Start Neovim and open buffers/windows/tabpages.\r\n- Decide that you want this to be saved as separate session.\r\n- Execute `:lua MiniSessions.write('my-new-session')`. This will save current state in a global session named \"my-new-session\".\r\n- Close Neovim.\r\n\r\nTo later restore \"my-new-session\" in a fresh Neovim instance, open Neovim (preferably with `autoread = false`) and execute `MiniSessions.read('my-new-session')`. To get the overview of all available sessions to read, execute `:lua MiniSessions.select()` and select the session to read (how selection is done depends on user's `vim.ui.select()` implementation). Or use 'mini.starter' with the latest 5 sessions and choose one.\r\n\r\nHope this helps.",
